messages:
Alex: Hey folks — opened a PR that consolidates several fallback examples into a compact guidance page and swaps some long examples for SDK-first snippets. link: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/8420. Need a quick sanity check on the behaviour descriptions (429, timeouts, context-trim) and the sample code.
Priya: Thanks Alex, on it. Which snippets do you want to prioritize? the JS SDK retry pattern or the python context-trim helper?
Alex: Prioritize JS retry + graceful stream degrade; then python helper. The old page had 3 variants — I compressed them into a single canonical pattern + short alternatives.
Matt: Quick note from runtime: we shouldn't suggest toggling aggressive prefix cache TTL without a caveat. It can reduce latency but cause stale prefix responses for interactive apps. Put a short warning. :warning:
Priya: Good call. I'll add a 1-line caveat. Also we should call out the headers we expect from the API (Retry-After, X-RateLimit-Remaining) so SDKs can surface guidance.
Sarah: From infra/runtime perspective, the preferred surfaced signal for transient capacity is the 429 with header Retry-After in seconds. For throttling we sometimes use 429 without Retry-After (best-effort), so clients should back off with exponential + jitter if Retry-After missing.
Kevin: I'll update the JS snippet to show exponential backoff with jitter and use Retry-After when present. Proposed snippet: ```js
// minimal retry with Retry-After
async function callWithRetries(req, max=3){
  for(let i=0;i<max;i++){
    const res = await fetch('/v1/generate', req);
    if(res.status===429){
      const ra = res.headers.get('retry-after');
      if(ra) await wait(parseInt(ra)*1000);
      else await wait(Math.pow(2,i)*100 + jitter());
      continue;
    }
    return res;
  }
  throw new Error('exhausted retries');
}
```
Priya: That looks good, Kevin. Add a comment about not retrying non-idempotent requests unless you have server-side dedupe.
Alex: Added a short paragraph in the PR about idempotency and guidance for chat vs. generate endpoints.
Julia: DocsBot: PR 8420 updated — added section "Compact fallback patterns" and removed duplicate examples across /err-ref and /playbooks. CI link: https://ci.redwood-inference/internal/8420/checks.
Priya: CI flaky lint is failing on the codeblock formatting — the linter wants three backticks + language tag for the python helper. I'll fix and re-run.
Matt: Also, can we include a tiny flow diagram (ASCII or simple list) for routing fallback: primary model -> short-context fallback -> quantized variant -> regional fallback -> 429 degrade mode. Keeps ops folks happy.
Alex: Good idea. I added an ordered list in the PR. Kept it one-line per step.
Kevin: SDK owners: question — do we want to expose a built-in helper like `sdk.retrying.generate()` or keep it as sample code? My vote: sample for now, built-in later after we settle semantics.
Priya: Agreed. We can reference an experimental helper in a note but keep the snippets manual.
Sarah: One more runtime caveat: when recommending context trimming, call out that trimming should prefer removing system or non-essential context first, not user-visible history. That prevents surprising chat behavior.
Alex: Noted, added example of trimming order and a micro-snippet for token-count heuristics.
Priya: I have a small nit: the link to the Dedicated SLA doc is pointing to an internal draft. Replace with the public SLA link.
Alex: Done.
Matt: LGTM from infra side after the caveats and flow diagram.
Kevin: I'll drop a small Python snippet for context-trim heuristics tonight.
Priya: I'll run a final pass for voice/tone and accessibility (headings, alt text for the diagram).
Alex: Thanks everyone — tagging for final review before merge: @Priya @Matt @Kevin @Sarah. If no objections by EOD I'll merge and publish the short redirect from the old pages. :rocket:
DocsBot: Reminder — please add a changelog entry when merging: docs: compact fallback patterns (PR 8420).
Priya: Will do.
Alex: merging tomorrow unless blockers. Cheers.
